 "interpretationRules": {
  "encodedRules": [],
  "sourceGuidance": [
   {
    "title": "CFI Scoring & Interpretation",
    "text": "Scores consist of a total CFI score and two subscale scores. The total score ranges between 20 and 140, where higher scores indicate more cognitive flexibility.\nA normative percentile for the total score and subscales are calculated, comparing the respondents scores to a sample of university students (Dennis & Vander Wal, 2010). Percentiles help contextualise how the respondent scored in relation to a typical pattern of responding. For example, a percentile of 50 indicates the individual has more cognitive flexibility than 50 percent of the normal population. i.e. is average.\nPercentiles below approximately 25 represent clinically significant inflexibility, which would be important to target within cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT). According to the CBT framework, cognitive inflexibility underpins the development and maintenance of depression and anxiety.\nA graph is presented of average scores (between 1 and 7), indicating the typical response on the likert scale and normalising scores between subscales.\nThe two subscales measuring important aspects of cognitive flexibility are:\nAlternatives : measuring the ability to perceive multiple alternative explanations for life occurrences and human behaviour and the ability to generate multiple alternative solutions to difficult situations. Range = 13 to 91 Sum items 1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 13, 14, 16,18, 19, 20\nControl : measuring the tendency to perceive difficult situations as controllable. Range = 7 to 49 Sum items 2, 4, 7, 9, 11, 15, 17\nNote that items 2, 4, 7, 9, 11, & 17 are reverse scored.\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "reportScoringGuidance": [
   {
    "documentSha256": "d5f822096e081788e92f99fe0298346b66f7899641149ad0cf20286388f11062",
    "pageNumber": 2,
    "text": "Scores consist of a total CFI score and two subscale scores, a\nhigher score indicating more cognitive flexibility. \nAlternatives (sum items 1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 13, 14, 16,18, 19, 20):\nmeasuring the ability to perceive multiple alternative explanations for\nlife occurrences and human behaviour and the ability to generate\nmultiple alternative solutions to difficult situations. \nControl (sum items 2, 4, 7, 9, 11, 15, 17): measuring the tendency to\nperceive difficult situations as controllable.\nA normative percentile for the total score and subscales are\ncalculated, comparing the respondents scores to a sample of\nuniversity students (Dennis & Vander Wal, 2010). Percentiles help\ncontextualise how the respondent scored in relation to a typical\npattern of responding. For example, a percentile of 83 or less\nindicates the individual has more cognitive flexibility than 83 percent\nof the normal population.\nNote that items 2, 4, 7, 9, 11, & 17 are reverse scored.",
    "usage": "source_guidance_for_review_not_executable_scoring",
    "runtimeEnabled": false
   }
  ],
  "sourceRequirements": [
   "A normative percentile for the total score and subscales are calculated, comparing the respondents scores to a sample of university students (Dennis & Vander Wal, 2010).",
   "Percentiles help contextualise how the respondent scored in relation to a typical pattern of responding.",
   "For example, a percentile of 50 indicates the individual has more cognitive flexibility than 50 percent of the normal population.",
   "Percentiles below approximately 25 represent clinically significant inflexibility, which would be important to target within cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT).",
   "A graph is presented of average scores (between 1 and 7), indicating the typical response on the likert scale and normalising scores between subscales."
  ],
